2435: // epoll/kqueue once a socket op is serviced cross-loop) a timed op's I/O and its
2436: // timeout timer can complete on different loops at once, which the fix makes
2437: // safe. It stresses:
2438: //
2439: //   * concurrent full-duplex - each connection's reader and writer run as
2440: //     separate tasks that may land on different executors, so recv and send for
2441: //     the same fd are driven from different loops at once;
2442: //   * migration mid-connection - each echo handler spawns+joins a tiny task
2443: //     every round, bouncing across executors, so one socket's reads/writes move
2444: //     between loops over the connection's life;
2445: //   * fd reuse across loops - many short connections in waves, so closed fd
2446: //     numbers get reused by new sockets, possibly on different loops.
2447: //
2448: // A lost wakeup (the failure mode of a per-loop readiness backend on a fd whose
2449: // loop changed) would stall the op until its finite timeout, which is counted as
2450: // an error; std.testing.allocator also fails on any leak, exercising the shared
2451: // table teardown.
